Scott’s opening claim is that constructs on neighbourhood systems, which he calls functors, build new domains from old, and that the product is the outstanding one and has the expected properties in this category; for notation the token sets \(\Delta_0\) and \(\Delta_1\) of \(\mathcal{D}_0\) and \(\mathcal{D}_1\) are assumed disjoint, with no loss of generality because \(\mathcal{D}_1\) may be replaced by an isomorphic system whose tokens are disjoint from \(\Delta_0\). The closure never defines a functor and never builds that isomorphic copy. `prodNbhd` forms the product neighbourhood of \(X\) and \(Y\) as the union of the left copy of \(X\) and the right copy of \(Y\) on the disjoint union of the token types, so the tagging is the disjointness. `prod` is Definition 3.1: the neighbourhoods are those products, the master is the product of the masters and is nonempty because the first master is, a consistent intersection splits by the componentwise intersection and inclusion laws, and every neighbourhood lies in the master because each factor does. Scott’s identity (1) is `prodNbhd_subset_iff`, identity (2) is `prodNbhd_inter`, and splitting law (3) is `prod_mem_split`, whose reverse direction intersects the two master slices and cancels the extra master factors by the inclusions into the masters. `pair`, `fst`, and `snd`, with `pair_fst_snd`, `fst_pair`, and `snd_pair`, are the element pairing and its components; `prodEquiv` is Proposition 3.2 and `pair_le_pair_iff` is 3.2(i), proved by padding a neighbourhood with the other master and invoking injectivity of `prodNbhd`. `proj₀`, `proj₁`, and `paired` are Definition 3.3; `proju2080_comp_paired` and `proju2081_comp_paired` are the two projection identities of Proposition 3.4, `paired_proj` is the uniqueness of a map into the product, and `toElementMap_paired` is the elementwise pairing, each by comparing neighbourhoods after the preimage identity. Those four statements are the universal property the opening calls the